clueweb09-en0004-18-02978.130 lump. Answer: A Lipoma is a benign fatty lump. They are very common in older, middle-aged dogs. Overweight female dogs are especially prone to developing Lipomas. They can also appear in cats and horses, but not as often. Lipomas are usually just under the skin, but they can be locally invasive, meaning they are meshed with muscle or connective tissue. Lipomas can also have additional blood or connective tissue as part of the growth. These growths can appear anywhere on the body, but they are most frequently located on the belly (mid-chest and down) and upper legs. Your vet may want to do a needle aspirate to further examine the lump and determine conclusively that it is a lipoma versus another type of growth. A needle aspirate is when a sterile needle is inserted into the lump and the plunger withdrawn, providing suction to collect cells from the lump. This
clueweb09-en0004-18-02978.260 sterile needle is inserted into the lump and the plunger withdrawn, providing suction to collect cells from the lump. This is not painful, and not usually even noticed by most pets. Your vet will then place the collected cells on a microscrope slide, stain them, and take a look under the microscope. Once your veterinarian has performed a needle aspirate and is certain that it is indeed a Lipoma, most vets recommend a watch-and-wait approach. The lump should be checked at regular intervals, to make sure there haven't been any cellular changes. Large lumps, especially those under a limb or in another location to interfere with movement or function, should be removed as soon as possible. It should be noted that there is a fatty tumor called a liposarcoma, and that is malignant. Metastasis is rare, but due to their nature (infiltrative) they are difficult to fullly remove, and recurrence
clueweb09-en0004-18-02978.390 that is malignant. Metastasis is rare, but due to their nature (infiltrative) they are difficult to fullly remove, and recurrence is common. More Veterinary Medicine Q&A Related Articles Veterinary Q & A - Lipomas (fatty tumors) in Dogs and Cats Veterinary Q & A - Should I Call The Vet? Part III My pet is "not herself" lately. Should I watch her, or call the v... Find a Vet to See Your Pet The Viewer Viewpoint - "Money Hungry Vets" vs "Proper" ... [Janet Tobiassen Crosby, DVM] Guide since 1999 Janet Tobiassen Crosby, DVM Veterinary Medicine Guide Sign up for my Newsletter My Blog My Forum Explore Veterinary Medicine Must Reads Pet Care Videos Vet Career Questions? Is My Pet Too Fat? How to Get Rid of Fleas Litter Box Issues Most Popular Veterinary Q & A ArchiveCan you give pets aspirin?Dry Nose - Health Problem?Canine PregnancyDog licking paws See
